CAND-0000109,ZPC-000385,,10.1166/jnn.2013.6011,Two-photon induced photopolymerization using photoinitiator with an intramolecular radical quenching moiety for nanolithography,experimental_context,wavelength_nm=780;pulse_width_fs=100;repetition_rate_MHz=82;power_measurement_plane=not_reported;threshold_definition=candidate_in_anchor;resin_formulation=candidate_in_anchor,context,,,,"ermore, the molar ratio of photoinitiators BNBC, BNBC/PhOCH 3 (molar ratio 1:1) and BNMBC were increased to 0.1% and the corresponding photoresists R 3, R4 and R5 were prepared to verify the radical quench- ing effect, in which the molar ratio of MMA, PE-3A were 92.35 and 7.55%, respectively. In the TPIP experiment, a mode-locked Ti:Sapphire laser system (Tsunami, Spectra-Physics) with a center wave- length of 780 nm, a pulse width of 100 fs, and a repe- tition rate of 82 MHz was employed as the laser source. The laser beam was tightly focused by a 100 × oil- immersion objective lens with a high numerical aperture (N.A. = 1.45, Olympus). The resin was drop-casted onto a cover glass substrate for 30 minutes before the fabri- cation. The laser beam was tightly focused into the liq- uid photopolymerisable resin on the cover glass. The ﬁnal structures were fabricated by a preprogrammed soft- ware controlled by computer. Then the unpolymerized resins were removed by ethanol. The fabricated structures were observed by a scanning electron microscope (SEM, S-4300, Hitachi, Japan). 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION The TPIP properties of BNBC and BNMBC were eval- uated by fabricating lines with deigned photoresists R 1 and R2. The polymerization threshold power ( Pth/rparenorifor pho- toresists were i","ermore, the molar ratio of photoinitiators BNBC, BNBC/PhOCH 3 (molar ratio 1:1) and BNMBC were increased to 0.1% and the corresponding photoresists R 3, R4 and R5 were prepared to verify the radical quench- ing effect, in which the molar ratio of MMA, PE-3A were 92.35 and 7.55%, respectively. In the TPIP ex