CAND-0000105,ZPC-000385,,10.1166/jnn.2013.6011,Two-photon induced photopolymerization using photoinitiator with an intramolecular radical quenching moiety for nanolithography,pi_loading,0.02,%,,,,"13, 1343–1346, 2013Two-Photon Induced Photopolymerization Using Photoinitiator with an Intramolecular Radical Quenching Moiety for Nanolithography Wei-Er Lu1/commaori2, Wei-Qiang Chen 1, Mei-Ling Zheng 1, Xian-Zi Dong 1, Zhen-Sheng Zhao1, and Xuan-Ming Duan 1/commaori∗ 1Laboratory of Organic NanoPhotonics and Key Laboratory of Functional Crystals and Laser Technology, Technical Institute of Physics and Chemistry, Chinese Academy of Sciences, No. 29, Zhongguancun East Road, Beijing, 100190, P . R. China 2Graduate University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100190, P . R. China We have investigated the photoinitiating properties of the photoinitiator 3,6-bis[2-(4-nitrophenyl)- ethynyl]-9-(4-methoxy-benzyl)-carbazole (BNMBC), which has an intramolecular radical quenching group “p-methoxybenzyl, ” in the substrate lines fabrication of two-photon induced photopolymer- ization (TPIP). Another photoinitiator 3,6-bis[2-(4-nitrophenyl)-ethynyl]-9-benzyl-carbazole (BNBC) with similar chemical structure but not radical quenching group was studied for comparison. Their photopolymerization properties were studied with resins in which BNBC and BNMBC were used as photoinitiators with a molar ratio of 0.02%, respectively . The linewidth of polymer lines fabricated by TPIP of the photoresist that conta","ole (BNBC) with similar chemical structure but not radical quenching group was studied for comparison. Their photopolymerization properties were studied with resins in which BNBC and BNMBC were used as photoinitiators with a molar ratio of 0.02%, respectively . The linewidth of polymer lines fabricated by TPIP of the photoresist that conta",,,,numeric_candidate,machine_numeric_extraction,machine_medium,auto_retained_low_context_candidate,automatic_candidate_with_context_warning,two_photon_numeric_extraction,CAND-0000105,,loading_not_normalizable,loading candidate; mmol/g only filled when directly reportable from text
CAND-0000107,ZPC-000385,,10.1166/jnn.2013.6011,Two-photon induced photopolymerization using photoinitiator with an intramolecular radical quenching moiety for nanolithography,pi_loading,0.1,%,,,,"ermore, the molar ratio of photoinitiators BNBC, BNBC/PhOCH 3 (molar ratio 1:1) and BNMBC were increased to 0.1% and the corresponding photoresists R 3, R4 and R5 were prepared to verify the radical quench- ing effect, in which the molar ratio of MMA, PE-3A were 92.35 and 7.55%, respectively. In the TPIP experiment, a mode-locked Ti:Sapphire laser system (Tsunami, Spectra-Physics) with a center wave- length of 780 nm, a pulse width of 100 fs, and a repe- tition rate of 82 MHz was employed as the laser source. The laser beam was tightly focused by a 100 × oil- immersion objective lens with a high numerical aperture (N.A. = 1.45, Olympus). The resin was drop-casted onto a cover glass substrate for 30 minutes before the fabri- cation. The laser beam was tightly focused into the liq- uid photopolymerisable resin on the cover glass. The ﬁnal structures were fabricated by a preprogrammed soft- ware controlled by computer. Then the unpolymerized resins were removed by ethanol. The fabricated structures were observed by a scanning electron microscope (SEM, S-4300, Hitachi, Japan). 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION The TPIP properties of BNBC and BNMBC were eval- uated by fabricating lines with deigned photoresists R 1 and R2. The polymerization threshold power ( Pth/rparenorifor pho- toresists were i","ermore, the molar ratio of photoinitiators BNBC, BNBC/PhOCH 3 (molar ratio 1:1) and BNMBC were increased to 0.1% and the corresponding photoresists R 3, R4 and R5 were prepared to verify the radical quench- ing effect, in which the molar ratio of MMA, PE-3A were 92.35 and 7.55%, respectively. In the TPIP experiment, a mode-locked Ti:Sapphire laser sy",,,,numeric_candidate,machine_numeric_extraction,machine_medium,auto_retained_low_context_candidate,automatic_candidate_with_context_warning,two_photon_numeric_extraction,CAND-0000107,,loading_not_normalizable,loading candidate; mmol/g only filled when directly reportable from text
CAND-0000108,ZPC-000385,,10.1166/jnn.2013.6011,Two-photon induced photopolymerization using photoinitiator with an intramolecular radical quenching moiety for nanolithography,pi_loading,7.55,%,,,,"ermore, the molar ratio of photoinitiators BNBC, BNBC/PhOCH 3 (molar ratio 1:1) and BNMBC were increased to 0.1% and the corresponding photoresists R 3, R4 and R5 were prepared to verify the radical quench- ing effect, in which the molar ratio of MMA, PE-3A were 92.35 and 7.55%, respectively. In the TPIP experiment, a mode-locked Ti:Sapphire laser system (Tsunami, Spectra-Physics) with a center wave- length of 780 nm, a pulse width of 100 fs, and a repe- tition rate of 82 MHz was employed as the laser source. The laser beam was tightly focused by a 100 × oil- immersion objective lens with a high numerical aperture (N.A. = 1.45, Olympus). The resin was drop-casted onto a cover glass substrate for 30 minutes before the fabri- cation. The laser beam was tightly focused into the liq- uid photopolymerisable resin on the cover glass. The ﬁnal structures were fabricated by a preprogrammed soft- ware controlled by computer. Then the unpolymerized resins were removed by ethanol. The fabricated structures were observed by a scanning electron microscope (SEM, S-4300, Hitachi, Japan). 3. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION The TPIP properties of BNBC and BNMBC were eval- uated by fabricating lines with deigned photoresists R 1 and R2. The polymerization threshold power ( Pth/rparenorifor pho- toresists were i","nitiators BNBC, BNBC/PhOCH 3 (molar ratio 1:1) and BNMBC were increased to 0.1% and the corresponding photoresists R 3, R4 and R5 were prepared to verify the radical quench- ing effect, in which the molar ratio of MMA, PE-3A were 92.35 and 7.55%, respectively. In the TPIP experiment, a mode-locked Ti:Sapphire laser system (Tsunami, Spectra-Physics) with a center wave- length of 780 nm, a pulse width of 100 fs, and a repe- tition rate of 82 MHz was employed as the laser source. The",,,,numeric_candidate,machine_numeric_extraction,machine_medium,auto_retained_low_context_candidate,automatic_candidate_with_context_warning,two_photon_numeric_extraction,CAND-0000108,,loading_not_normalizable,loading candidate; mmol/g only filled when directly reportable from text
CAND-0000110,ZPC-000385,,10.1166/jnn.2013.6011,Two-photon induced photopolymerization using photoinitiator with an intramolecular radical quenching moiety for nanolithography,pi_loading,65,%,,,,"resulted in the decrease of the weakly-polymerized region, thus the resolution of TPIP was improved. Furthermore, when the photoinitiator had quenching moiety, like BNMBC, was introduced into photoresist, high efﬁciencies of rad- icals quenching and chain terminating could be achieved due to the more collision chances between intramolecu- lar quenching group and radicals in a short time than that of BNBC/PhOCH 3 system, which also led to the higher TPIP resolution. As a result, the narrowest linewidth was obtained from the resin with BNMBC as photoinitiator compared to other photoinitiating systems studied here. 4. CONCLUSIONS In summary, a photoinitiator BNMBC with an intramolec- ular radical quenching group “ p-methoxybenzyl” was investigated in TPIP by comparing with a similar photoini- tiator BNBC without radical quenching group. The TPIP fabrication experiments exhibited that the BNMBC pos- sessed high TPIP initiating efﬁciency and effective radical quenching effect. The linewidth of polymer lines fabri- cated by TPIP of the photoresist using BNMBC as the photoinitiator could be decreased to 65% of those using BNBC. Moreover, this intramolecular radical quenching reaction exhibited more effective conﬁning effect of radi- cal diffusion compared to intermolecular radical quencher with the","ents exhibited that the BNMBC pos- sessed high TPIP initiating efﬁciency and effective radical quenching effect. The linewidth of polymer lines fabri- cated by TPIP of the photoresist using BNMBC as the photoinitiator could be decreased to 65% of those using BNBC. Moreover, this intramolecular radical quenching reaction exhibited more effective conﬁning effect of radi- cal diffusion compared to intermolecular radical quencher with the",,,,numeric_candidate,machine_numeric_extraction,machine_medium,auto_retained_low_context_candidate,automatic_candidate_with_context_warning,two_photon_numeric_extraction,CAND-0000110,,loading_not_normalizable,loading candidate; mmol/g only filled when directly reportable from text
CAND-0000113,ZPC-000385,,10.1166/jnn.2013.6011,Two-photon induced photopolymerization using photoinitiator with an intramolecular radical quenching moiety for nanolithography,pi_loading,0.1,%,,,,"e introduced a radical quencher, phenyl methyl ether (PhOCH 3/rparenori, to BNBC and further studied the photopolymerization properties by using BNBC, BNBC/PhOCH 3 and BNMBC as pho- toinitiators with a molar ratio of 0.1%. The results further indicated that BNMBC was effective to conﬁne the radical diffusion in polymerization due to the radical quenching effect of moiety . This intramolecular radical quenching moiety exhibited more effective conﬁning effect of radical diffusion compared to the intermolecular radical quencher PhOCH 3. Furthermore, arbitrary complex three- dimensional (3D) microstructure was achieved using the photoresist with photoinitiator BNMBC. The approach could open up broad prospect for improving the resolution in TPIP . Keywords: T wo-Photon Induced Photopolymerization, Photoinitiator, Radical Quenching Moiety , Nanolithography , Intramolecular Radical Quenching Effect. 1. INTRODUCTION The micro/nanofabrication based on two-photon induced photopolymerization (TPIP) has been extensively studied and established as a powerful and unique tool in the past decade 1/commaori2 due to its particular advantageous for making structures of almost any complexity at the micro/nano scale which was difﬁcult to be achieved using other meth- ods. Recent efforts have been made to improve t","e introduced a radical quencher, phenyl methyl ether (PhOCH 3/rparenori, to BNBC and further studied the photopolymerization properties by using BNBC, BNBC/PhOCH 3 and BNMBC as pho- toinitiators with a molar ratio of 0.1%. The results further indicated that BNMBC was effective to conﬁne the radical diffusion in polymerization due to the radical quenching effect of moiety . This intramolecular radical quenching moiety exhibited more effective conﬁning effect",,,,numeric_candidate,machine_numeric_extraction,machine_medium,auto_retained_low_context_candidate,automatic_candidate_with_context_warning,two_photon_numeric_extraction,CAND-0000113,,loading_not_normalizable,loading candidate; mmol/g only filled when directly reportable from text
CAND-0000117,ZPC-000385,,10.1166/jnn.2013.6011,Two-photon induced photopolymerization using photoinitiator with an intramolecular radical quenching moiety for nanolithography,pi_loading,0.1,%,,,,"obtained from the top and 45 /compBview, respectively. High resolution and the complex 3D structure with massive overhangs such as the crossed columns of the bird’s nest can be observed clearly. J. Nanosci. Nanotechnol. 13, 1343–1346, 2013 1345 --- Page 4 --- Delivered by Ingenta to: Nanyang Technological University IP: 46.161.63.210 On: Wed, 15 Jun 2016 23:56:11 Copyright: American Scientific Publishers RESEARCH ARTICLE TPIP Using Photoinitiator with an Intramolecular Radical Quenching Moiety for Nanolithography Lu et al. Fig. 4. SEM images of a bird’s nest fabricated from photoresist R5 con- taining 0.1% BNMBC as the photoinitiator with a laser power of 8.1 mW at the scanning speed of 66 /SLmums −1 . Based on the experimental results, we have proposed a possible mechanism on conﬁning radical diffusion in TPIP using different photoinitiating systems. The density of two- photon induced radicals was distributed depending on the intensity of the laser focused beam when the photoresist was exposed to the laser. The center of the laser spot was a molecular weight densely-polymerized region that formed following the laser irradiation, which turned into solid state. Closing to the solid state was a weakly-polymerized region with low molecular weight which determined the resolution after developi","yright: American Scientific Publishers RESEARCH ARTICLE TPIP Using Photoinitiator with an Intramolecular Radical Quenching Moiety for Nanolithography Lu et al. Fig. 4. SEM images of a bird’s nest fabricated from photoresist R5 con- taining 0.1% BNMBC as the photoinitiator with a laser power of 8.1 mW at the scanning speed of 66 /SLmums −1 . Based on the experimental results, we have proposed a possible mechanism on conﬁning radical diffusion in TPIP using different photoinitiatin",,,,numeric_candidate,machine_numeric_extraction,machine_medium,auto_retained_low_context_candidate,automatic_candidate_with_context_warning,two_photon_numeric_extraction,CAND-0000117,,loading_not_normalizable,loading candidate; mmol/g only filled when directly reportable from text
