zotero_3UHK5MQX:threshold:6,zotero_3UHK5MQX,3UHK5MQX,10.1002/adom.201901040,On the schwarzschild effect in 3D two-photon laser lithography,polymerization_threshold,,,,,3,"ture remains after the process.[4] Naively, the product of the square of the local laser intensi ty and the exposure time determines the local degree of photo -induced crosslinking. In disagreement with this expectation, for laser intensities and hence the laser powers below a certain threshold, the monomer is insufficiently crosslinked, even for extre mely long exposure times. This behavior is the two-photon laser-lithography analogue of the (one-photon) Schwarzschild effect. The origin of this behavior, which has been observed for many different commonly used photoinitiators and monomers, has previousl y been traced back to oxygen diffusion .[2, 5, 6 ]","ture remains after the process.[4] Naively, the product of the square of the local laser intensi ty and the exposure time determines the local degree of photo -induced crosslinking. In disagreement with this expectation, for laser intensities and hence the laser powers below a certain threshold, the monomer is insufficiently crosslinked, even for extre mely long exposure times. This behavior is the two-photon laser-lithography analogue of the (one-photon) Schwarzschild effect. The origin of this behavior, which has been observed for many different commonly used photoinitiators and monomers, has previousl y been traced back to oxygen diffusion .[2, 5, 6 ]",,,,anchor_candidate,machine_text_anchor,text_exact_candidate,auto_anchor_candidate,automatic_field_discovery,anchor_mining,zotero_3UHK5MQX:threshold:6,outputs/zotero_tpp_literature_pilot_20260828/parsed_local_pdfs/texts/zotero_3UHK5MQX_10.1002_adom.201901040.txt,Keyword/field anchor mined from parsed full text; value extraction was not independently asserted.,pending
