zotero_D6JVVDG4:isc_t1:13,zotero_D6JVVDG4,D6JVVDG4,,"An Evidence-Guided, Mechanism-Aware Workflow for Functional Molecular Materials Discovery: Two-Photon Photoinitiators as a Demonstration",isc_t1_proxy,,,,,9,"rom the radical headline rather than being mixed into a photoinitiator ranking. Putative Type-I can- didates require evidence for accessible excited-state activation, relevant bond cleavage and chemically credible radical fragments. Type-II candidates require triplet-state characterization and an explicit co-initiator model, because the candidate molecule alone does not define the complete radical-generating system. SET/PET exploratory candidates require donor–acceptor pairing and excited-state electron-transfer thermo- dynamics rather than optical charge-transfer character alone. 9","rom the radical headline rather than being mixed into a photoinitiator ranking. Putative Type-I can- didates require evidence for accessible excited-state activation, relevant bond cleavage and chemically credible radical fragments. Type-II candidates require triplet-state characterization and an explicit co-initiator model, because the candidate molecule alone does not define the complete radical-generating system. SET/PET exploratory candidates require donor–acceptor pairing and excited-state electron-transfer thermo- dynamics rather than optical charge-transfer character alone. 9",,,,anchor_candidate,machine_text_anchor,text_exact_candidate,auto_anchor_candidate,automatic_field_discovery,anchor_mining,zotero_D6JVVDG4:isc_t1:13,outputs/zotero_tpp_literature_pilot_20260828/parsed_local_pdfs/texts/zotero_D6JVVDG4.txt,Keyword/field anchor mined from parsed full text; value extraction was not independently asserted.,pending
zotero_D6JVVDG4:isc_t1:14,zotero_D6JVVDG4,D6JVVDG4,,"An Evidence-Guided, Mechanism-Aware Workflow for Functional Molecular Materials Discovery: Two-Photon Photoinitiators as a Demonstration",isc_t1_proxy,,,,,10,"t files were submit-ready or checkpoint-dependent at the manifest stage. For Type-I candidates, bond dissocia- tion, excited-state localization, radical stability and downstream radical reactivity are the central questions. For Type-II and SET/PET candidates, triplet energetics, co- initiator compatibility and electron-transfer driving forces become more important. This separation ensures that a candidate is evaluated against the evidence required by its proposed mechanism. 10","t files were submit-ready or checkpoint-dependent at the manifest stage. For Type-I candidates, bond dissocia- tion, excited-state localization, radical stability and downstream radical reactivity are the central questions. For Type-II and SET/PET candidates, triplet energetics, co- initiator compatibility and electron-transfer driving forces become more important. This separation ensures that a candidate is evaluated against the evidence required by its proposed mechanism. 10",,,,anchor_candidate,machine_text_anchor,text_exact_candidate,auto_anchor_candidate,automatic_field_discovery,anchor_mining,zotero_D6JVVDG4:isc_t1:14,outputs/zotero_tpp_literature_pilot_20260828/parsed_local_pdfs/texts/zotero_D6JVVDG4.txt,Keyword/field anchor mined from parsed full text; value extraction was not independently asserted.,pending
zotero_D6JVVDG4:mechanism:1,zotero_D6JVVDG4,D6JVVDG4,,"An Evidence-Guided, Mechanism-Aware Workflow for Functional Molecular Materials Discovery: Two-Photon Photoinitiators as a Demonstration",mechanism,,,,,1,"ly plausi- ble initiation. Here we present an evidence-guided, mechanism-aware workflow for functional molecular materials discovery, using radical two-photon photoini- tiators as a demonstration. Literature-derived rules define photoinitiator scope, separate Type-I, Type-II and SET/PET candidates from cationic and bound- ary cases, and constrain multitask molecular learning. Scaffold-isolated testing gave comparable six-task performance for two directed message-passing mod- els (macroR 2 = 0.8415and 0.8411), while benchmark and family audits exposed both productive enrichment and OXE-family concentration. Applied to literature-derived derivatives and ZINC22-scale accessible sp","ly plausi- ble initiation. Here we present an evidence-guided, mechanism-aware workflow for functional molecular materials discovery, using radical two-photon photoini- tiators as a demonstration. Literature-derived rules define photoinitiator scope, separate Type-I, Type-II and SET/PET candidates from cationic and bound- ary cases, and constrain multitask molecular learning. Scaffold-isolated testing gave comparable six-task performance for two directed message-passing mod- els (macroR 2 = 0.8415and 0.8411), while benchmark and family audits exposed both productive enrichment and OXE-family concentration. Applied to literature-derived derivatives and ZINC22-scale accessible sp",,,,anchor_candidate,machine_text_anchor,text_exact_candidate,auto_anchor_candidate,automatic_field_discovery,anchor_mining,zotero_D6JVVDG4:mechanism:1,outputs/zotero_tpp_literature_pilot_20260828/parsed_local_pdfs/texts/zotero_D6JVVDG4.txt,Keyword/field anchor mined from parsed full text; value extraction was not independently asserted.,pending
zotero_D6JVVDG4:mechanism:2,zotero_D6JVVDG4,D6JVVDG4,,"An Evidence-Guided, Mechanism-Aware Workflow for Functional Molecular Materials Discovery: Two-Photon Photoinitiators as a Demonstration",mechanism,,,,,1,"i- ble initiation. Here we present an evidence-guided, mechanism-aware workflow for functional molecular materials discovery, using radical two-photon photoini- tiators as a demonstration. Literature-derived rules define photoinitiator scope, separate Type-I, Type-II and SET/PET candidates from cationic and bound- ary cases, and constrain multitask molecular learning. Scaffold-isolated testing gave comparable six-task performance for two directed message-passing mod- els (macroR 2 = 0.8415and 0.8411), while benchmark and family audits exposed both productive enrichment and OXE-family concentration. Applied to literature-derived derivatives and ZINC22-scale accessible space, the","i- ble initiation. Here we present an evidence-guided, mechanism-aware workflow for functional molecular materials discovery, using radical two-photon photoini- tiators as a demonstration. Literature-derived rules define photoinitiator scope, separate Type-I, Type-II and SET/PET candidates from cationic and bound- ary cases, and constrain multitask molecular learning. Scaffold-isolated testing gave comparable six-task performance for two directed message-passing mod- els (macroR 2 = 0.8415and 0.8411), while benchmark and family audits exposed both productive enrichment and OXE-family concentration. Applied to literature-derived derivatives and ZINC22-scale accessible space, the",,,,anchor_candidate,machine_text_anchor,text_exact_candidate,auto_anchor_candidate,automatic_field_discovery,anchor_mining,zotero_D6JVVDG4:mechanism:2,outputs/zotero_tpp_literature_pilot_20260828/parsed_local_pdfs/texts/zotero_D6JVVDG4.txt,Keyword/field anchor mined from parsed full text; value extraction was not independently asserted.,pending
