zotero_ZI5SFNAQ:threshold:15,zotero_ZI5SFNAQ,ZI5SFNAQ,10.1002/adom.202200474,Photoinitiator-free two-photon polymerization of biocompatible materials for 3D micro/nanofabrication,polymerization_threshold,,,,,13,"zed tyrosine one (Figure S8), indicating that visible-wavelength two-photon excitation onto collagen induced photo-oxidation of tyrosine residues. Figure 4G shows the plot of the Raman peak intensity at 1772 cm-1. The plot shows a drastic increase above a threshold intensity of 141 kW/cm2. It has been reported that the appearance of the C=O peak at approximately 1780 cm-1 is due to the partial hydrolysis of peptide links within collagen[57]. It is also known that UV exposure to collagen induces the cleavage of its peptide bonds[58–60]. These facts indicate that the cleavage of peptide bonds in collagen was induced in DUV-TPP. Raman intensities and auto-fluorescence intens","zed tyrosine one (Figure S8), indicating that visible-wavelength two-photon excitation onto collagen induced photo-oxidation of tyrosine residues. Figure 4G shows the plot of the Raman peak intensity at 1772 cm-1. The plot shows a drastic increase above a threshold intensity of 141 kW/cm2. It has been reported that the appearance of the C=O peak at approximately 1780 cm-1 is due to the partial hydrolysis of peptide links within collagen[57]. It is also known that UV exposure to collagen induces the cleavage of its peptide bonds[58–60]. These facts indicate that the cleavage of peptide bonds in collagen was induced in DUV-TPP. Raman intensities and auto-fluorescence intens",,,,anchor_candidate,machine_text_anchor,text_exact_candidate,auto_anchor_candidate,automatic_field_discovery,anchor_mining,zotero_ZI5SFNAQ:threshold:15,outputs/zotero_tpp_literature_pilot_20260828/parsed_local_pdfs/texts/zotero_ZI5SFNAQ_10.1002_adom.202200474.txt,Keyword/field anchor mined from parsed full text; value extraction was not independently asserted.,pending
